#
# Replacement for /etc/logrotate.d/rsyslog — 2026-10-29 (Phase 3 remediation).
#
# Changes vs Ubuntu default (which rotates weekly / rotate 4):
#   - daily rotation
#   - size 100M threshold — rotate if the file reaches 100 MB before daily
#   - rotate 7 generations (≈ 1 week of history)
#   - compress + delaycompress preserved (.1 stays uncompressed for tail/grep)
#   - **ADDED `su root syslog`** — logrotate 3.19.0 refuses to rotate
#     files whose parent directory is group-writable unless the config
#     explicitly declares the privileged user. /var/log is 0775
#     root:syslog on this box, which triggers the check. `su root syslog`
#     tells logrotate to run file operations as uid=root gid=syslog so
#     ownership is preserved AND the parent-dir check is satisfied.
#     Reference: logrotate commit 54e6f8c (parent-dir mode check,
#     security tightening).
#
# Rotation stays IN-PLACE on root fs. Decision rationale: rsyslog's postrotate
# hook sends SIGHUP, which reopens the renamed syslog file. Using `olddir`
# across filesystems (to /mnt/volume_lon1_1778405456698/) would require
# `copytruncate` instead of rename, which has a brief duplicate-write window
# that is acceptable but unnecessary when the in-place footprint is bounded.
# With rotate 7 and ~15 MB compressed-generation size, root-fs ceiling is
# ~ (100 MB active) + (90 MB × 6 compressed) ≈ 200 MB, vs the previous
# weekly worst case of ~700 MB.
#
# File ownership context (verified 2026-10-29):
#   /var             drwxr-xr-x root   root     0755
#   /var/log         drwxrwxr-x root   syslog   0775   ← group-writable, triggers check
#   /var/log/syslog  -rw-r----- syslog adm      0640   ← will be preserved by `create` default
#
# `su root syslog` is the standard Ubuntu pattern for this directory
# — matches the directory's owner:group pair exactly.
#
# If the operator later wants compressed .gz generations to live on the
# attached volume, add a weekly archive sweep (systemd timer) that moves
# /var/log/syslog.*.gz → /mnt/.../syslog_archive/ once each file is ≥ 7
# days old.
#
# Dry-run before deploying:
#   sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/rsyslog
#
/var/log/syslog
/var/log/mail.info
/var/log/mail.warn
/var/log/mail.err
/var/log/mail.log
/var/log/daemon.log
/var/log/kern.log
/var/log/auth.log
/var/log/user.log
/var/log/lpr.log
/var/log/cron.log
/var/log/debug
/var/log/messages
{
	rotate 7
	daily
	maxsize 100M
	su root syslog
	missingok
	notifempty
	compress
	delaycompress
	sharedscripts
	postrotate
		/usr/lib/rsyslog/rsyslog-rotate
	endscript
}
